1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,540
Your heart pumps more blood in half an
hour than your whole body holds.

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:11,007
For fourteen hundred years, doctors taught
that blood was made fresh from food and

3
00:00:11,027 --> 00:00:12,000
used up.

4
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:15,320
So how did a band tied round an arm prove
them wrong?

5
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:21,511
By the end of this film, you'll know how
one London doctor proved that your blood

6
00:00:21,531 --> 00:00:26,500
circulates, with a sum, a band, and tiny
doors hidden inside your veins.

7
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:29,600
Every university taught the old idea.

8
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:31,680
Every doctor believed it.

9
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:35,500
If it was wrong, medicine had the human
body backwards.

10
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:37,740
London, sixteen-sixteen.

11
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:43,210
A short, dark-eyed doctor called William
Harvey starts teaching anatomy at the

12
00:00:43,230 --> 00:00:45,000
Royal College of Physicians.

13
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:48,740
He will spend the next twelve years on one
question.

14
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:51,220
Where does all that blood go?

15
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:55,560
And to answer it, he did something doctors
almost never did.

16
00:00:55,580 --> 00:00:56,700
He counted.

17
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:05,052
Galen was a Greek doctor in Rome nearly
two thousand years ago, and a brilliant

18
00:01:05,072 --> 00:01:06,072
anatomist.

19
00:01:06,333 --> 00:01:10,213
For fourteen centuries, hardly anyone
dared to argue with his books.

20
00:01:11,013 --> 00:01:12,333
Here is what Galen taught.

21
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:14,133
You eat.

22
00:01:14,293 --> 00:01:19,353
Your food turns into a milky juice, and
your liver turns that juice into blood.

23
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:25,125
Your veins carry the blood out to every
part of you, and your flesh soaks it up as

24
00:01:25,145 --> 00:01:26,145
nourishment.

25
00:01:26,553 --> 00:01:28,773
The heart wasn't a pump at all.

26
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:32,973
It was a warm stove, and its job was to
heat the blood.

27
00:01:33,753 --> 00:01:38,119
A little of it was meant to seep from the
right side of the heart to the left,

28
00:01:38,139 --> 00:01:40,613
through tiny holes in the wall between
them.

29
00:01:41,213 --> 00:01:42,993
Nobody had ever seen those holes.

30
00:01:43,993 --> 00:01:45,853
Doctors believed they had to be there
anyway.

31
00:01:46,513 --> 00:01:51,943
Then, in fifteen fifty-five, the great
anatomist Andreas Vesalius wrote that he

32
00:01:51,963 --> 00:01:56,793
could not see how even the smallest drop
could pass through that wall.

33
00:01:57,613 --> 00:01:59,933
So why did everyone keep believing it?

34
00:02:00,473 --> 00:02:02,793
Because nobody had a better answer.

35
00:02:03,533 --> 00:02:08,627
Around sixteen hundred, in Padua, a young
Englishman named William Harvey studied

36
00:02:08,647 --> 00:02:11,173
under an old professor called Fabricius.

37
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:18,793
Fabricius had found something strange
inside the veins: tiny flaps, in pairs.

38
00:02:19,453 --> 00:02:21,053
He called them little doors.

39
00:02:21,853 --> 00:02:23,713
But why would a vein need doors?

40
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:28,913
Fabricius thought they slowed the blood,
so it wouldn't all pool in your feet.

41
00:02:29,613 --> 00:02:34,433
He held the most important clue in the
story, and he read it the wrong way.

42
00:02:35,093 --> 00:02:36,833
Harvey took the clue home to London.

43
00:02:39,613 --> 00:02:41,973
Picture yourself in his lecture room in
London.

44
00:02:42,793 --> 00:02:46,753
It's cold, the candles are lit, and you're
on a hard wooden bench.

45
00:02:47,553 --> 00:02:52,741
Harvey has spent years watching the slow
hearts of cold-blooded animals, like frogs

46
00:02:52,761 --> 00:02:55,333
and eels, where each beat is easy to see.

47
00:02:55,573 --> 00:02:57,233
And what he saw was simple.

48
00:02:57,893 --> 00:02:59,173
The heart isn't a stove.

49
00:02:59,573 --> 00:03:02,213
It's a muscle, and it squeezes.

50
00:03:02,893 --> 00:03:08,161
Each squeeze pushes blood out into the
arteries, and that push is the pulse you

51
00:03:08,181 --> 00:03:09,453
feel in your wrist.

52
00:03:10,113 --> 00:03:13,313
Then he asks how much blood each squeeze
pushes out.

53
00:03:13,893 --> 00:03:15,933
He picks small numbers on purpose.

54
00:03:16,593 --> 00:03:19,553
Say the left side of the heart holds about
two ounces.

55
00:03:20,293 --> 00:03:23,253
Say just one eighth of that goes out with
each beat.

56
00:03:23,993 --> 00:03:27,913
In half an hour, a heart beats more than a
thousand times.

57
00:03:28,533 --> 00:03:29,613
Multiply it out.

58
00:03:30,153 --> 00:03:35,333
In half an hour, the heart pushes out more
blood than there is in the whole body.

59
00:03:35,933 --> 00:03:36,993
Why does that matter?

60
00:03:37,733 --> 00:03:42,120
Because if the liver made all that blood
fresh from food, you'd have to eat more

61
00:03:42,140 --> 00:03:44,233
than your own weight every single day.

62
00:03:44,993 --> 00:03:45,993
Nobody does.

63
00:03:46,333 --> 00:03:49,153
And if the body used it all up, where
would it go?

64
00:03:49,793 --> 00:03:51,413
There's only one answer that works.

65
00:03:52,013 --> 00:03:53,233
It's the same blood.

66
00:03:53,853 --> 00:03:58,380
The heart pumps it out through the
arteries, and it comes back through the

67
00:03:58,400 --> 00:03:59,813
veins, round and round.

68
00:04:00,373 --> 00:04:01,433
It circulates.

69
00:04:02,313 --> 00:04:07,506
We now know he was being very careful:
your heart pumps all of your blood round

70
00:04:07,526 --> 00:04:09,373
your body in about a minute.

71
00:04:10,093 --> 00:04:11,793
But a sum isn't proof.

72
00:04:12,753 --> 00:04:15,473
Harvey needed something people could see
with their own eyes.

73
00:04:18,313 --> 00:04:22,445
Harvey showed his experiments at the
College for more than nine years before he

74
00:04:22,465 --> 00:04:23,465
printed a word.

75
00:04:23,893 --> 00:04:26,713
The most famous one needs nothing but a
strip of linen.

76
00:04:27,413 --> 00:04:31,093
He ties a band round a man's upper arm and
pulls it tight.

77
00:04:31,813 --> 00:04:36,330
Below the band, the pulse is gone, and
slowly the hand grows cool.

78
00:04:36,910 --> 00:04:39,030
Above it, the artery throbs.

79
00:04:39,610 --> 00:04:40,610
Why?

80
00:04:40,630 --> 00:04:43,170
The tight band has stopped the blood
getting in.

81
00:04:43,610 --> 00:04:45,370
Then he loosens it, just a little.

82
00:04:46,010 --> 00:04:51,290
Now the arteries, which run deep inside
the arm, can push blood under the band.

83
00:04:51,930 --> 00:04:55,070
But the veins near the skin are still
squeezed shut.

84
00:04:55,690 --> 00:05:00,210
The hand flushes red, and the veins below
the band swell up like ropes.

85
00:05:01,170 --> 00:05:05,250
So blood comes in through the arteries and
leaves through the veins.

86
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:07,370
Now the little doors.

87
00:05:08,070 --> 00:05:11,030
Along the swollen veins you can see small
knots.

88
00:05:11,690 --> 00:05:15,290
Each one is a valve, the flaps Fabricius
found.

89
00:05:16,250 --> 00:05:20,430
Harvey presses a finger on a vein and
pushes the blood towards the hand.

90
00:05:21,030 --> 00:05:23,150
It stops dead at the next valve.

91
00:05:23,810 --> 00:05:28,210
He pushes it the other way, towards the
heart, and it slides through easily.

92
00:05:28,830 --> 00:05:32,270
He empties a short stretch of vein between
two fingers.

93
00:05:32,790 --> 00:05:36,410
It stays empty until he lifts the finger
nearer the hand.

94
00:05:36,850 --> 00:05:39,410
Then it fills straight back up, from
below.

95
00:05:40,170 --> 00:05:44,910
Every door in every vein opens one way:
towards the heart.

96
00:05:46,390 --> 00:05:47,790
Fabricius thought they slowed the blood.

97
00:05:48,870 --> 00:05:50,830
Harvey saw they were steering it home.

98
00:05:51,590 --> 00:05:53,150
You can see a little of this yourself.

99
00:05:53,990 --> 00:05:55,890
Look at the veins on the back of your
hand.

100
00:05:56,470 --> 00:05:59,630
The blood inside them is on its way back
to your heart.

101
00:06:00,270 --> 00:06:05,927
In sixteen twenty-eight, Harvey published
it all in a small Latin book, printed in

102
00:06:05,947 --> 00:06:09,270
Frankfurt: On the Motion of the Heart and
Blood.

103
00:06:10,030 --> 00:06:11,250
Not everyone was impressed.

104
00:06:12,130 --> 00:06:17,531
John Aubrey, who knew him, later wrote
that after the book came out, Harvey "fell

105
00:06:17,551 --> 00:06:22,350
mightily in his practice", and many people
thought he was crack-brained.

106
00:06:22,370 --> 00:06:24,850
And one gap was still open.

107
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:26,510
Harvey knew it.

108
00:06:28,830 --> 00:06:31,470
Harvey's circle had a hole in it.

109
00:06:31,850 --> 00:06:35,310
How did the blood get from the arteries
into the veins?

110
00:06:36,350 --> 00:06:40,970
Harvey guessed the blood passed through
tiny gaps in the flesh, too small to see.

111
00:06:41,470 --> 00:06:42,570
He never saw them.

112
00:06:43,090 --> 00:06:48,570
He died in sixteen fifty-seven, with his
great idea still missing one piece.

113
00:06:49,310 --> 00:06:54,484
Four years later, in Bologna, a doctor
called Marcello Malpighi looked at the

114
00:06:54,504 --> 00:06:57,270
thin lung of a frog through a microscope.

115
00:06:58,010 --> 00:06:59,110
And there it was.

116
00:06:59,630 --> 00:07:05,721
A net of tiny vessels, finer than hairs,
carrying blood from the smallest arteries

117
00:07:05,741 --> 00:07:07,530
into the smallest veins.

118
00:07:08,250 --> 00:07:11,670
We call them capillaries, from the Latin
word for hair.

119
00:07:12,250 --> 00:07:13,610
The circle was closed.

120
00:07:14,350 --> 00:07:18,950
Your blood leaves your heart, runs out
through your arteries, crosses through the

121
00:07:18,970 --> 00:07:21,650
capillaries, and comes home through your
veins.

122
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:24,570
So why was this so groundbreaking?

123
00:07:25,610 --> 00:07:30,345
Because once you know the blood goes
round, you know that anything put into one

124
00:07:30,365 --> 00:07:32,050
vein reaches the whole body.

125
00:07:32,770 --> 00:07:37,370
Within ten years of Harvey's death,
doctors in England and France were trying

126
00:07:37,390 --> 00:07:39,130
the first blood transfusions.

127
00:07:39,790 --> 00:07:43,330
That makes no sense unless your blood
circulates.

128
00:07:44,030 --> 00:07:48,190
And the next time a nurse wraps a cuff
round your arm, think of Harvey.

129
00:07:49,010 --> 00:07:50,950
A blood pressure cuff is his band.

130
00:07:51,610 --> 00:07:56,459
It squeezes until the blood stops getting
in, then lets go, and listens for the

131
00:07:56,479 --> 00:07:58,390
moment it starts to flow again.

132
00:07:59,150 --> 00:08:02,550
A sum, a band, and a row of little doors.

133
00:08:03,430 --> 00:08:08,251
Harvey proved that your heart is a pump
and that your blood goes round, long

134
00:08:08,271 --> 00:08:10,310
before anyone saw the last link.

135
00:08:11,530 --> 00:08:16,412
Thirteen years later, a Dutch cloth
merchant with an even better lens looked

136
00:08:16,432 --> 00:08:19,850
into a drop of pond water, and found it
full of life.

137
00:08:20,630 --> 00:08:22,690
That is where the story of germs begins.
